<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Plock</h1><p class="st-meta">1275 CE – 1495 CE (approx.) · capital Plock</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northern Masovian duchy around the old Piast city of Plock, a Polish vassal until its incorporation in 1495.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1275 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-masovia">Duchy of Masovia</a> was split, and the part centred on Plock, an early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast</a> capital and bishopric on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-ii-of-masovia">Boleslaw II of Masovia</a>. For two centuries Plock was sometimes reunited with the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> and sometimes ruled as a separate duchy.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Plock was an old episcopal city with a Romanesque cathedral containing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast</a> tombs. Its dukes balanced between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Bohemia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-of-plock">Wenceslaus of Plock</a> did homage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-bohemia">John of Bohemia</a> in 1329. From 1351 the Masovian dukes were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Polish Crown.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last duke of Plock, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janusz-ii-of-plock">Janusz II</a>, died in 1495, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-albert">King John I Albert</a> incorporated the duchy into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1025-1385">Kingdom of Poland</a> (1385-1569). The rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a> followed in 1526.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="duchy, 1275 CE – 1495 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">duchy</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.07%;width:4.38%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1275 CE – 1495 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Plock</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Boleslaw II of Masovia, Wenceslaus of Plock, Janusz II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Polish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Plock</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1385&amp;focus=duchy-of-plock" aria-label="Duchy of Plock on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5115 385 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Plock lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5442 621h0M5457 692h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5435 641h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5435 641h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Poland. <a href="/atlas?year=1385&amp;focus=duchy-of-plock">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
